## INTRODUCTION
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed from metal cations or clusters and organic ligands, are an intriguing new class of crystalline hybrid materials with reticular topology. The fascinating characteristics of high surface areas, predesignable pore size, homogeneously dispersed active sites, and versatile functionalities make MOFs very attractive candidates for gas storage, molecule separation, drug delivery, heterogeneous catalysis, sensing, etc.2−8 Especially, the vast number of combinations of metal centers and linkers provide structural and functional diversity for MOFs, which is considered as the unique and competitive advantage of MOFs over previously known porous materials, like activated carbon and zeolite.
Many researchers are dedicated to functional design, controllable synthesis, and precise characterization of MOFs and their structure−property relationship investigation as well as potential application exploration, and a series of major breakthroughs have been achieved over the past decades. $\angle-4,9-11^{\circ}$ Along with this, there is an ever-growing demand for processing MOF nanocrystals into specific devices required in practical application scenarios.12,13 However, MOFs synthesized from traditional techniques, like a solvothermal method, are commonly fine powders or tiny particles with intrinsic brittleness, insolubility, and nonthermoplastic property. They can hardly be shaped by using typical solvent and melt processing techniques. The poor processability of MOFs significantly hinders their extensive applications. It is urgently needed to develop controllable and scalable processing approaches to shape MOFs into desired configurations (e.g., films, foams, etc.), while still preserving the individual merits of MOFs. Recently there are an increasing number of processability studies of MOFs and also impressive work on the application of MOF films, foams, or other shaped bodies .12−15 Environmental remediation has become one of the most important applications of MOFs since they proved to be efficient adsorbents or catalysts for health hazard removal from air and water.16−18 In this Account, we present our recent progress in MOF film fabrication and focus on the interesting contributions of these porous films to environmental decontamination.
STRATEGIES FOR MOF FILM FABRICATION According to Fischer and co-workers,15 MOF films can be divided into two main categories, surface-mounted MOFs (SURMOFs) and polycrystalline films. The former is a kind of ultrathin and smooth MOF film newly emerging.19 Highly ordered, preferentially oriented, and thickness-tunable SURMOFs can be elaborately designed and achieved under carefully controlled conditions by using methods like the Langmuir−Blodgett technique and liquid phase epitaxy.20 By contrast, polycrystalline films are usually thick films with individual MOF particles randomly distributed in a matrix or on a surface discretely or continuously. Here, we mainly focus on the polycrystalline MOF films, which are more commonly used in environmental pollution control. Up to now, a variety of methods have been employed to produce such a kind of MOF films, including solvothermal synthesis, microwaveassisted deposition, seeded growth, electrochemical synthesis, dip-coating, freeze-drying, etc.13−15 Generally, two main concepts are introduced, including post-treatment of the performed MOF particles and in situ growth of MOF films. The first concept usually involves the transfer of the selected MOF particles onto a surface or into a matrix with or without the help of polymers.13,21−23 The integration of MOF and polymer is a very promising way which could endow MOF films with considerable flexibility and processability as well as new functions.21,24−27 On the down side, the activity of MOFs may be partly diminished due to the blockage of pores or channels by polymer chains.28,29 Also the films may lack homogeneity due to the aggregation of MOF particles.22,30 Direct growth of MOF nanocrystals on the given surface is a straightforward and useful strategy to obtain MOF coatings.14,31−35 However, some commonly used techniques, like solvothermal synthesis, usually could not lead to a firm bond between MOF layers and substrates. Also these methods may be time or energy consuming from the view of practical application. Each of the above strategies has its own benefits and drawbacks that we should make a balance of according to the practical needs. The following are several effective fabrication protocols we employed, including in situ interweaving, postsynthetic polymerization (PSP), and solvent-free hotpressing (HoP) methods we proposed (Figure 1), as well as electrospinning and freeze-drying methods.
## Incorporation of the Performed MOF Particles into Polymer Matrix
Starting from the first concept, here we present in situ interweaving, PSP, electrospinning and freeze-drying methods, respectively.25−27,36−38
In Situ Interweaving Method. Flexible and conductive MOF films, serving as electrodes for high-performance solidstate supercapacitors (SSC), were prepared by developing an in situ interweaving method.36 Briefly, isolated and insulating MOF crystals resting on a flexible substrate were electrochemically interwoven by intrinsically conducting polymers. The fabrication of an MOF-based electrode was a two-step process. First, ZIF-67 selected as an electrical charge storing material, was uniformly coated on the carbon cloth (CC) in the presence of Super $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ and a poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF) binder. By using the obtained ZIF-67-CC as a working electrode, conductive polyaniline (PANI) was then synthesized from monomer aniline via an electrochemical process to interconnect nonconductive ZIF-67 crystals (Figure 2a). The crystallinity of ZIF-67 was well maintained after these procedures. The resultant PANI-ZIF-67-CC with MOF loading as high as $3.5~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ was porous, but its specific surface area and pore volume were much lower than that of ZIF-67-CC and pristine ZIF-76. The polymer chains not only cover the outer surface of MOF but also go deep into the internal pore. This is helpful for the efficient electron migration along or accessing the backbone of MOF crystals (Figure 2b) and, consequently, brings a remarkable areal capacitance of $2146~\mathrm{mF~c\bar{m}^{-2}}$ at $10\ \mathrm{m}\bar{\mathrm{V}}\ s^{-1}$ .
Photoinduced Postsynthetic Polymerization. Since postsynthetic modification (PSM) was proposed by Cohen and others,39 it has been proven to be a very powerful strategy for the functionalization of MOFs. Inspired by PSM, we have developed PSP for controlled integration of the selected MOF particles and polymers.27 Stand-alone and homogeneous MOF films with good elasticity and flexibility can be obtained by PSP strategy. The two key steps in the PSP process are covalent modification of the organic ligand with polymeriziable functional groups and subsequent graft polymerization of organic monomers on MOFs. Compared with conventional polymerization techniques, photoinduced polymerization was employed due to its unique advantages, such as mild reaction condition, less side reaction, solvent-free process, and easy molding. As schematically illustrated in Figure 3a, MOF nanoparticles bearing initiator groups (UIO-66-NH-Met) were prepared by reacting methacrylic anhydride with amino groups in UIO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ . Then, the functionalized UIO-66-NH-Met nanocrystals copolymerized with acrylate monomers under UV within several minutes. As verified by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption (Figure 3b−d), homogeneous and porous UIO$66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{poly}$ (butyl methacrylate) (PBMA) hybrid films were successfully derived by the PSP method without destroying the framework of MOF. Compared with the UIO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ /PBMA blend membrane, the PSP-derived membrane shows no obvious particle aggregations and interface defects (Figure 3b), due to the enhancement of polymer−MOF compatibility by forming chemical interactions. The PSP strategy is also available to obtain the UIO-66-NH-Met/poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA) hybrid film. It is regarded as a general and effective approach. Dong and co-workers have prepared a flexible UIO-66-urea-based film with tunable MOF loadings for dye adsorption and separation by copolymerization of $\mathrm{UIO}{-}66{\-}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ particles with polyurethane monomers.40
Electrospinning Method. Besides postpolymerization, an alternative way for polymer incorporation is physical blending of MOF nanocrystals with polymers followed by electrospinning, freeze-drying, etc. Electrospinning is a useful and facile fiber fabrication technology, allowing for the production of MOF-based nanofibers.26,37,41 After being mixed with polyacrylonitrile (PAN), polystyrene (PS), or polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), four MOFs (ZIF-8, UIO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ MOF-199, and Mg-MOF-74), distinct from topologies and surface functionalities, were electrospun into either free-standing fiber mats or nanofibrous layers on the given substrates (Figure 4).26 The composite films can meet both high MOF loading (60 wt $\%$ ) and good MOF particles dispersion. As expected, the porosity as well as the surface area of polymer film were significantly increased with the addition of MOFs. For example, the ZIF-8/PAN hybrid had about a factor of 10 improvements in BET surface area over pure PAN. Benefiting from the advantages of both MOF and polymer, MOF-based nanofibrous layers supported on flexible substrates, like nonwoven fabrics, can serve as filters for air pollution control. We will discuss this in detail in the later application section.
Freeze-Drying Method. Polymers, sometimes, might decrease the porosities of MOFs and have a direct influence tcomtphoe anctt.i2v8,i2t9y,Oenseppercoiamlliys wg hweany iot aecttrs aosf ahni irnoabcltievme is to fabricate MOF/polymer composites into porous foams through freeze-drying.42 The freeze-drying procedure involves the direct removal of template solvents by sublimation. Its potential advantages are as follows. (1) Low-temperature processing is applicable for some MOFs with poor thermostability. (2) The MOF/polymer hybrid could be easily molded into the designed shape without being influenced by the elimination of solvent molecules. (3) The unfavorable blockage of the MOF pore by polymer chains can be weakened by creating additional macro-pores and/or meso-pores via freeze-drying. The hierarchical pore structure thus formed is beneficial for the effective mass transfer in gas or liquid applications.
Because of these advantages, a freeze-drying technique has been performed on MOF composites in a few studies.42 We embarked on shaping MOF nanocrystals into robust and functional foam films by employing such a powerful approach. Specifically, HKUST $\ @\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ foams with tunable MOF loadings were generated by freeze-drying of the fluid mixture of $\mathrm{HKUST}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC) in a mold with a cylinder shape.38 The thus-obtained composite foams exhibit a hierarchical micromeso porous structure, low volume density $\left(<0.1\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\right)$ , certain magnetism, along with good mechanical stability and high robustness. Especially, the robustness of the foam was fully proven by a pressure resistance test, scotch tape test, and collision test. The freezedrying method applies to ZIF-8, MOF-74, and UIO-66-based foam fabrication as well. Due to the gradient pore distribution, a conversion efficiency of $92\%$ and selectivity of $97\%$ were realized in $\mathrm{C-H}$ activation reactions by using HKUST $\ @\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ foam.
The MOF-based foams reported are usually limited to bulky bodies with only one type of MOF incorporated. The advantages of MOFs in structural and functional diversity are not efficiently explored, especially in mixed-component applications. It is still a big challenge to process various MOFs with different functionalities into one applicable and shape-desired device in an efficient way. The templated freezedrying strategy offers the possibility of flexible and easy fabrication of MOF devices. The effectiveness and versatility of this method were shown by the preparation of various types of hollow tubular MOF devices (Figure 5).25 Uniform and regular hollow tubes of five typical MOFs (ZIF-8, UIO-66, $\mathrm{U}\mathrm{\bar{I}O}{-}66{\-}{\mathrm{N}}\mathrm{H}_{2},$ MOF-74, and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\cdot$ ) were simply prepared by freezing the aqueous suspension of MOFs and sodium alginate (SA) in a mold with a cylinder template in the middle at $77~\mathrm{K},$ followed by freezing-drying after peeling off the template. The resulted hollow tubes have controllable MOF loadings and tunable hole-diameters, which could be adjusted from 30 to 70 wt $\%$ and from 4.5 to $8.0\ \mathrm{\mm},$ , respectively. Also, these tubes are highly robust, due to the chemical interactions between the functional groups (e.g., −COOH and $-\mathrm{OH},$ ) rich in SA and open metal sites and defects existing in MOFs. Its robustness was well proven by a series of compressive tests under the condition of different MOF loadings. Moreover, SA shows a slight influence to the porous structure of MOFs. For example, ZIF-8@SA-HT (50 wt $\%$ ) and MIL-101- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{SA-HT}}$ (50 wt $\%$ ) both maintain the advantages of the two corresponding MOFs in specific surface areas (579 and $924~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\dot{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , respectively) and pore volumes (0.462 and $0.615~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , respectively). More importantly, multicomponent MOF foams with conjugated, “Janus”, or “coaxial” tubular structures could be obtained by applying a templated freeze-drying protocol. Particularly, through a layer-by-layer fashion, ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with distinct functionalities could be gathered tightly to form multilayer coaxial hollow tubes for mixed pollutants capture. ZIF-8@SA-HT with “cellular” structures can be used as high efficiency air filters for PM removal, similar to cellular ceramic filters.
## In Situ Growth of MOF Films
Direct self-assembly of MOF precursors on the given surface is a straightforward and very promising strategy for the fabrication of functional MOF coating layers. It can be achieved by methods like solvothermal synthesis, electrochemical deposition, low-temperature self-assembly, etc.14,43−45 Solvothermal synthesis is one of the most commonly employed approaches. In this process, the precursors not only react at the substrate/solution interface but also in the solution phase at the same time. The latter is unfavorable. Also the above fabrication process may be time or energy consuming, and the resulted MOF coatings may lack stability due to the weak affinity of MOF particles to the substrate surface. Alternatively, the HoP process without the use of both solvent and polymer binder was proposed to realize in situ production of continuous and robust MOF coatings speedily and efficiently.35 We have further extended this method to a large-scale roll-to-roll production technique.46
Solvent-Free HoP Method. Stable MOF layers on either rigid or flexible substrates were generated by HoP-powdered precursor mixtures (metal salts and organic linkers) in the presence of polyethylene glycol (PEG) under $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 10 min with an electric iron.35 The following two steps are involved in the HoP process. First, under the given temperature and pressure, metal ions or ligands were chemically bonded to the substrates with abundant functional groups or metal sites on the surface (e.g., cloth, metal foils) (Figure 6). Then, nucleation and subsequent MOF crystal growth occur on the substrate. PXRD, SEM, and elemental mapping characterizations confirmed the successful generation of a dense layer of MOF nanocrystals on the substrates. PEG that was very effective in promoting the diffusion of precursors was removed completely from MOF layers after synthesis. By employing the HoP method, various kinds of MOFs, such as ZIF-8, ZIF-67, ZIF-9, Co-MOF, Cd-MOF, and mixed-metal MOF Ni-ZIF-8, could be uniformly and continuously coated on the commercially available substrates, such as carbon cloth, nickel foam, glass fiber, and so on. The abrasion resistance test and mechanical stirring experiment confirmed that significant improvement in the robustness of MOF coatings has been achieved by adopting the HoP approach. It is noteworthy, that the functional diversity of MOF layers could also be enhanced by developing layer-by-layer MOF coatings or “Janus” films via the HoP strategy. These advantages offer the obtained MOF coatings in good practicability. For example, excellent adsorption performance for toxic ions in water was realized by the MOF $@$ Carbon cloth. Also, the HoP method has some limitations that need to be addressed in the future, such as the nonapplicability to high-valent MOFs and the uncontrollable aggregation of MOF particles on the supports.
Roll-to-Roll Processing Method. Roll-to-roll processing, a mass production technique for MOF coatings, has been developed from the solvent-free HoP method.46 Under the working conditions of $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and a rotating speed of $15\ \mathrm{rpm},$ ZIF-8 nanocrystals were readily in situ grown on five commercial substrates with different working temperatures, including plastic mesh $\left(80-100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)$ , glass cloth $(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , metal mesh $(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , nonwoven fabric $(150-250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , and melamine foam $(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , respectively (Figure 7). The generated ZIF-8 nanocrystals preserved their characteristic ofa microporous structure. ZIF-8 loading as well as particle size could be controlled by changing HoP cycles. PXRD measurements confirmed the thermal stability of these MOF films at their realistic working temperatures. A series of durability tests, such as bending, twisting, rubbing, and pressing tests, proved the strong attachment of MOF particles to the substrate surface. ZIF-8 coatings on readily available substrates can be potentially treated as filters and applied for particulate matters (PM) capture in industrial and residential environments.
## Environmental Applications of MOF Films
Environmental pollution has become one of the most serious problems worldwide. Contaminants, like PM, $\mathrm{~O}_{3},$ $S\mathrm{Ox}$ , $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{NOx},}\end{array}$ heavy metal ions, dyes, pathogens, etc., commonly existing in air or water, are major threats to human health. Adsorbents, such as activated carbon, zeolite, metal oxide, resin, and biomass, are usually employed to remove these pollutants from either air or water. However, most of these materials have the problems of limited adsorption capacity, particularly, for lowlevel pollutants, and high energy consumption of regeneration. MOFs with structural and functional diversity show huge potential for applications in the adsorption and catalytic degradation of environmental health hazards.16−18 Fabrication of MOF-based filters (denoted as MOFilters) is a major step to promote the practical application of MOFs in environmental pollution control.
MOFilters for PM Removal. PM pollution is one of the major concerns around the world, especially in developing countries. MOFs with unsaturated metal sites and defects are thought to be capable of removing highly polar PM due to their electrostatic interactions (Figure 8a). We explored the possibilities of MOFilters for efficient PM removal.25,26,46 We initially investigated the PM filtration performance of four kinds of electrospun nanofibrous MOFilters in both simulated and real smoggy environments in Beijing.26 These MOFilters all have PAN as a polymer matrix, varying in MOFs. Four MOFs with distinct zeta potential are selected, including MOF-199, UIO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ Mg-MOF-74, and ZIF-8 (Figure 8b). Under the condition of real hazy weather $\left(\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}=350\mu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{m}^{-3}\right.$ , $\mathrm{PM}_{10}=720\ \mu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{m}^{-3},$ $\mathrm{RH}=58.6\%$ , and $T=23.5\ {^{\circ}C};$ , the four MOFilters are all superior to PAN in PM removal efficiency. It is found, that the PM removal capability of the MOFilter is closely related to the zeta potential value of each MOF, except Mg-MOF-74. ZIF-8 has the highest zeta potential value among the four. Its filter shows the strongest ability for PM removal, with nearly $90\%$ efficiency achieved. After continuous exposure to a hazy environment for $^{48\mathrm{h},}$ , the ZIF-8/PAN filter shows no obvious performance degradation. Its weight was accordingly increased by $29.5\ \mathrm{g\m}^{-2}$ or $0.037\ \mathrm{g\g^{-1}}$ due to the attachment of PM particles on the fibrous filters.
MOFilters fabricated via electrospinning techniques usually have weak robustness, which is a serious drawback in practical applications. ZIF-8-based filters with high robustness have been produced by a roll-to-roll HoP process on a large-scale.46 Five kinds of MOFilters with different working temperatures (ranging from 80 to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) have been prepared (Figure 7b). In a simulated pipe filtration system (Figure 9a), these filters all show more than $90\%$ PM filtration efficiency along with a low pressure drop at a flow rate of $0.2\textrm{m s}^{-1}$ . ZIF-8@Melamine foam presents a long-lasting PM removal efficiency of ${>}95.4\%$ over $12\mathrm{h}$ (Figure 9b) and could be reused several times after a simple washing and drying process without significant loss of performance. The ZIF-8@Glass cloth and ZIF-8@Metal mesh with high endurable temperatures are available for the PM filtration at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This high-temperature performance endows the MOFilter with the possibility of being applied in industry environments. In simulated indoor conditions, the ZIF-8@Plastic mesh, with a high light transmittance and good air permeability, realized a 4-fold increase in PM removal efficiency compared with bare plastic mesh. It is noteworthy, that the excellent PM filtration performance of ZIF-8@Plastic mesh could last for over one month in real environments. Besides, the “cellular” PM filter was produced by freeze-drying of ZIF-8/SA composites, as discussed above (Figure $\mathsf{9c-e},$ ).25 The “cellular” filter presents remarkable PM removal efficiency $(\mathrm{PM}_{2.5},$ $92~\pm~2.2\%$ ; $\mathrm{PM}_{10},$ $95~\pm~2.6\%)$ along with a low pressure drop (e.g., $12\ \mathrm{\Pa}_{\mathrm{;}}$ , $10~\mathrm{cm}~\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ , and it might be potentially used as a diesel particulate filter in vehicles.
MOFilters for Harmful Gas Removal. $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is one of the main gas pollutants harmful to both human health and the biological system. Electrospun nanofibrous MOFilters are not only useful for PM removal but also for $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ capture.26 At an inlet concentration of $100\ \mathrm{ppm}$ and a flow rate of $50~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , the MOF-199/PAN filter and UiO ${-66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}/{\mathrm{PAN}}$ filter show a much stronger adsorption effect to $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ compared with the pure PAN filter, because of the abundant binding sites (open metal sites or amino groups) within MOFs. In addition, the pressure drop in the MOFilter is relatively small, maximally not exceeding $20\ \mathrm{Pa}$ at a flow rate of $50\mathrm{\mL\min^{-1}}$ (Figure 10a). UiO- $6\dot{6}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}/\mathrm{PAN}$ keeps a good adsorption capacity toward $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ after it has been continuously used several times (Figure 10b). Ground-level ozone is a major air pollutant commonly found surrounding us, especially during sunny days and in confined spaces, like cabins, offices, etc. We discovered that MIL-100(Fe), with the advantages of high surface area and spatially arranged Fe active sites, was an efficient catalyst for long-term decomposition of ozone.47 Furthermore, MOFilters were fabricated by coating MIL-100(Fe) particles onto nonwoven fabrics for low-concentration ozone elimination (Figure 10c,d). The 200 ppb of ozone could be completely decomposed by the MOFilter under the conditions of $\mathsf{i}000\dot{\mathrm{~mL~min}^{-1}}$ flow rate and $45\%$ RH. The excellent catalytic activity makes the MOFilter possible to be used as an active layer in masks for personnel protection against ozone contamination.
MOF-Based Films for Water Decontamination. Human production and life also inevitably cause pollution to water resources. The PSP-derived membrane as filter media was found to be very effective for toxic $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ ions removal.27 It gives a high first-cycle retention rate of $80\%$ and maximum adsorption capacity of $8{\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}}$ , superior to that of the UIO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ /PBMA blend membrane and activated carbon/PBMA blend membrane (Figure 11a). The adsorption performance of MOF-based tubular films for contaminants in the liquid phase was also investigated in our work.25 ZIF-8@SA hollow tubes are effective for removing ${\mathrm{AsO}}_{4}^{3-}$ ions from $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), and both ${\mathrm{AsO}}_{4}^{3-}$ and methyl orange (MO) in DMF solution could be trapped at the same time by doublelayer $(\mathrm{ZIF{-}8\ ~+~\ N H_{2}{-}M I L{-}\bar{1}\bar{0}1})@\mathrm{SA}$ hollow tubes (Figure $^{\mathrm{11b,c}},$ ). After being cross-linked with ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ ions, MOF/SA composite tubes become water stable and are available for water purification. Saturated capacity values of $51.9\ \mathrm{mg\thinspaceg^{-1}}$ for ${\mathrm{AsO}}_{4}^{3\bar{-}}$ and $152.7\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}$ for MO were obtained for crosslinked ZIF-8@SA-HT and cross-linked $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}@\mathrm{SA}{\cdot}$ HT, respectively.
## Concluding Remarks and Prospects
In this Account, we summarized our recent advances in controllable synthesis of MOF films and their environmental applications. We presented two strategies for polycrystalline MOF films fabrication, post-treatment of the performed MOF particles with polymers, and in situ growth of MOF layers on the substrates. Four methods, including postsynthetic polymerization, in situ interweaving, electrospinning, and freeze-drying, were introduced to achieve hybrid films with the integrated advantages of MOFs and polymers. Particularly, postsynthetic polymerization is enabled to achieve free-standing and homogeneous films due to the enhancement of polymerMOF compatibility by forming covalent interactions. Flexible and conductive MOF coatings could be produced by in situ interweaving isolated MOF particles resting on substrates via electropolymer. In situ hot-pressing and its related roll-to-roll production methods were also proposed to achieve stable MOF coatings on commercially available substrates (e.g., cloth and metal foils), where metal ions or ligands chemically bonded to the surface functional groups or metal sites at the early stage of nucleation are responsible for the robustness of MOF layers. Furthermore, we demonstrated the potential applications of these MOF films in air and water decontamination. Although significant progresses have been achieved, MOF thin film fabrication is still an emerging field full of energy and challenges. First, low-cost fabrication methods with generic applicability are largely desired, especially the solidstate synthesis techniques. Second, further detailed study is needed to access the formation process and mechanism of
MOF coatings or films, along with the interfacial interactions between MOF layers and the substrate surface. Third, it is preferred to derive hierarchical and multifunctional MOF films for the application in a complex environment by utilization of the designability of MOFs. Moreover, it is very promising to realize the precise control of the arrangement and orientation of MOF particles and the thickness of MOF films. Last but not the least, future work should give much attention to the integration of MOF films with other components in the device for practical application.
